[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089648] [:db/retract 17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089649] [:db/retract 17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089650] [:db/retract 17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089646] [:db/retract 17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089647] [:db/retract 17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089536] [:db/retract 17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089532] [:db/retract 17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089533] [:db/retract 17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089534] [:db/retract 17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089535] {:db/id 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" "question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" "question-f4cb9239-3b4d-5884-8d60-86ece34323bd" "question-a7dfbbbd-1a36-5131-be23-c3a22a696cb3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091934 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n![](images/f4/f44326b7be98378285978658be508da822c110d5d61e8950872cc75c4296d7ae.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-de58daf1-e39b-5442-89f0-7a159aadbac7" "question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" "question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091935 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2 .$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have $\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7,$ and $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36+7-18 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(5,25).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" "question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" "question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" "question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091936 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2,X_3]^T$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n![](images/12/129ff41cb0fc5917efdc4f990d3d2efeb7546911eadb52340156f1ddc4f9d92f.svg)\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/id #uuid "33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/math-academy-id "q-159742" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=5\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4=9.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{9}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=6\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" "answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" "answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" "answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" "answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/id #uuid "929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/id #uuid "63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/id #uuid "bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/id #uuid "f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/id #uuid "a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/id #uuid "c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159744" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=20\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot (-6)=64.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{64}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=16\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" "answer-9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbba0c48627c" "answer-84f8a713-083b-567a-87f3-b56ca285c20f" "answer-246df32d-4746-5885-a035-d11664f353a2" "answer-d25bb8b0-15e2-5a36-ad57-03625d178a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/id #uuid "3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbba0c48627c" :answer/id #uuid "9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbba0c48627c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84f8a713-083b-567a-87f3-b56ca285c20f" :answer/id #uuid "84f8a713-083b-567a-87f3-b56ca285c20f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246df32d-4746-5885-a035-d11664f353a2" :answer/id #uuid "246df32d-4746-5885-a035-d11664f353a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d25bb8b0-15e2-5a36-ad57-03625d178a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d25bb8b0-15e2-5a36-ad57-03625d178a3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4cb9239-3b4d-5884-8d60-86ece34323bd" :question/id #uuid "f4cb9239-3b4d-5884-8d60-86ece34323bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159743" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=2,\\,\\rho =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=20\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{36}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=12\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e8b48ff-d943-5ecb-9098-05bbf34d8cc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e8b48ff-d943-5ecb-9098-05bbf34d8cc8" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e8b48ff-d943-5ecb-9098-05bbf34d8cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" "answer-593fcdbb-3bf7-51f3-8353-2a0bb906561b" "answer-e51b59d3-e3d9-56a2-bc23-364e27227cef" "answer-cd5b40e6-2599-5535-a953-6e2b8d5c5bcb" "answer-1ea11954-6a4f-5d76-99d3-931555d6a5a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" :answer/id #uuid "44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-593fcdbb-3bf7-51f3-8353-2a0bb906561b" :answer/id #uuid "593fcdbb-3bf7-51f3-8353-2a0bb906561b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e51b59d3-e3d9-56a2-bc23-364e27227cef" :answer/id #uuid "e51b59d3-e3d9-56a2-bc23-364e27227cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd5b40e6-2599-5535-a953-6e2b8d5c5bcb" :answer/id #uuid "cd5b40e6-2599-5535-a953-6e2b8d5c5bcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ea11954-6a4f-5d76-99d3-931555d6a5a9" :answer/id #uuid "1ea11954-6a4f-5d76-99d3-931555d6a5a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092579 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=10\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 5=25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{25}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=10\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089645 :answer-field/correct "answer-59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" "answer-0d4c0cb8-d9a4-5c3a-9a2d-8f3bcc6f2d8b" "answer-fca68fd5-18c4-580f-8af1-5f191fa77ca9" "answer-bb0c62a2-2941-508f-8b34-49fd4599dbf4" "answer-843df82d-bd20-5e2b-8054-24ab30500319"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" :answer/id #uuid "59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d4c0cb8-d9a4-5c3a-9a2d-8f3bcc6f2d8b" :answer/id #uuid "0d4c0cb8-d9a4-5c3a-9a2d-8f3bcc6f2d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fca68fd5-18c4-580f-8af1-5f191fa77ca9" :answer/id #uuid "fca68fd5-18c4-580f-8af1-5f191fa77ca9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb0c62a2-2941-508f-8b34-49fd4599dbf4" :answer/id #uuid "bb0c62a2-2941-508f-8b34-49fd4599dbf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-843df82d-bd20-5e2b-8054-24ab30500319" :answer/id #uuid "843df82d-bd20-5e2b-8054-24ab30500319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7dfbbbd-1a36-5131-be23-c3a22a696cb3" :question/id #uuid "a7dfbbbd-1a36-5131-be23-c3a22a696cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159737" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=2\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{4}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-47b850ea-8926-5c8f-8acd-249aeec872ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-47b850ea-8926-5c8f-8acd-249aeec872ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "47b850ea-8926-5c8f-8acd-249aeec872ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df425403-018d-5628-b418-23b7c205731c" "answer-0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" "answer-e0f879ab-0152-5dc2-bd80-fa45cb37146f" "answer-638f0ce8-9e40-5e97-b676-b714876cc977" "answer-ca644bff-bc2d-5268-9f93-15a088f842f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df425403-018d-5628-b418-23b7c205731c" :answer/id #uuid "df425403-018d-5628-b418-23b7c205731c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" :answer/id #uuid "0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f879ab-0152-5dc2-bd80-fa45cb37146f" :answer/id #uuid "e0f879ab-0152-5dc2-bd80-fa45cb37146f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-638f0ce8-9e40-5e97-b676-b714876cc977" :answer/id #uuid "638f0ce8-9e40-5e97-b676-b714876cc977" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca644bff-bc2d-5268-9f93-15a088f842f6" :answer/id #uuid "ca644bff-bc2d-5268-9f93-15a088f842f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092580 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\ge 1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-2)}{\\sqrt{13}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.832)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-P(Z<0.83) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-Φ(0.83) \\\\  & =1-0.7967 \\\\  & =0.2033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.2033.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089651 :answer-field/correct 17592186089655 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-de58daf1-e39b-5442-89f0-7a159aadbac7" :question/id #uuid "de58daf1-e39b-5442-89f0-7a159aadbac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159776" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\le 10).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2{\\mu }_{1}+4{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =2(-2)+4(3) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}(2)+{4}^{2}(4)+2(2)(4)(-1) \\\\  & =8+64-16 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,56).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le 10).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.27)=0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 10)=0.6064.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c46b911-92e9-5ee5-b90b-d6b9b56e65d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c46b911-92e9-5ee5-b90b-d6b9b56e65d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c46b911-92e9-5ee5-b90b-d6b9b56e65d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c21487c-b2e7-534d-af44-5ceea08e6498" "answer-67dbfefa-4caa-5758-9ac2-e1c248073a63" "answer-6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" "answer-bfbb04af-60be-5e8f-b9e6-9d32e8c07a07" "answer-82b2c84e-fc23-5e11-8368-7f6cfde6c81d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c21487c-b2e7-534d-af44-5ceea08e6498" :answer/id #uuid "8c21487c-b2e7-534d-af44-5ceea08e6498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5636" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67dbfefa-4caa-5758-9ac2-e1c248073a63" :answer/id #uuid "67dbfefa-4caa-5758-9ac2-e1c248073a63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5987" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" :answer/id #uuid "6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6064" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfbb04af-60be-5e8f-b9e6-9d32e8c07a07" :answer/id #uuid "bfbb04af-60be-5e8f-b9e6-9d32e8c07a07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6368" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82b2c84e-fc23-5e11-8368-7f6cfde6c81d" :answer/id #uuid "82b2c84e-fc23-5e11-8368-7f6cfde6c81d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6443" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/id #uuid "3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159778" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$\n\n*Hint: Tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1587\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3409\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3372\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3336\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+3\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+3\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =5+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+45-12 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,34).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 5) & =P(\\frac{1-8}{\\sqrt{34}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{5-8}{\\sqrt{34}}) \\\\  & =P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z<-1.20) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z\\le -1.20) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.20) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.51) & =0.3050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20) \\\\  & =0.3050-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.1899.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(1\\le Y\\le 5)=0.1899.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" "answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" "answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" "answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" "answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/id #uuid "45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1558" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/id #uuid "1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1762" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/id #uuid "e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1899" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/id #uuid "07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2002" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/id #uuid "23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2109" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092560 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089531 :answer-field/correct "answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" "answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" "answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" "answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" "answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/id #uuid "22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/id #uuid "2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,17)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/id #uuid "dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/id #uuid "09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{15})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/id #uuid "9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{17})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/id #uuid "485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159747" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2+4+2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(3,8).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/id #uuid "31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" "answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" "answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" "answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" "answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/id #uuid "1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/id #uuid "483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(3,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/id #uuid "275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(3,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/id #uuid "b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(6,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/id #uuid "f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(6,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/id #uuid "a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159906" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+3+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-1+14 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(4,19).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/id #uuid "53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" "answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" "answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" "answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" "answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/id #uuid "52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(2,21)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/id #uuid "458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(4,19)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/id #uuid "407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(4,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/id #uuid "1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(6,19)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/id #uuid "b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(6,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092581 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y<1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+2-2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+10-1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(3,12).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y<1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<1) & =P(Z<\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58) & =P(Z\\le -0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.58)=0.7190.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.58)=1-Φ(0.58)=1-0.7190=0.2810.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58)=0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y<1)=0.2810.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089657 :answer-field/correct 17592186089659 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/id #uuid "5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/math-academy-id "q-159788" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2-2-3 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9-5+60 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-3,64).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/id #uuid "67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" "answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" "answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" "answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" "answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/id #uuid "b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-1,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/id #uuid "9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-3,64)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/id #uuid "bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-3,8)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/id #uuid "ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(2,64)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/id #uuid "a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(2,71)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/id #uuid "b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159794" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+9+4 \\\\  & =13,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+42+30 \\\\  & =67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(13,67).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" "answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" "answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" "answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" "answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/id #uuid "4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(13,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/id #uuid "0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(13,67)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/id #uuid "9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(17,51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/id #uuid "21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(5,34)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/id #uuid "ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(5,67)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/id #uuid "279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159790" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" "answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" "answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" "answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" "answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/id #uuid "8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-7,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/id #uuid "212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(-7,25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/id #uuid "e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(1,25)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/id #uuid "96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(4,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/id #uuid "85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N(4,13)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093481 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be two (dependent) random variables. We say that $\\mathbf{X}$ is a **bivariate normal vector** with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ if the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, \n\n- $\\sigma^2_1 = \\textrm{Var}[X_1]$ and $\\sigma^2_2 = \\textrm{Var}[X_2]$ are the variances of $X_1$ and $X_2,$\n\n- $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]$ is the covariance of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n\n- $\\rho$ is the linear correlation coefficient of $X_1$ and $X_2,$ and\n\n- $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma,$ the covariance matrix.\n\nA graph of a typical density function of a bivariate normal vector is depicted below. \n\n![](images/d1/d195358d6dc5b7b8a1080d53c774ee4289ce0f97c17de3bf45a52e86c6b20e29.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is often referred to as having a \"bell shape.\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093483 :tutorial/content "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-1}|Σ{|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-2/2}{|Σ|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2),$ we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093485 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ \n\n- when $\\rho \\to 0,$ the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, while\n\n- when $\\rho \\to 1,$ the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n \n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$\n\n- when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" while\n\n- for larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2,$ the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093484 :tutorial/content "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as *\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093482 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T$ with mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T$ with this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors: \n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as \n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\n is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092422 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186091934 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$"] [17592186091934 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n![](images/f4/f44326b7be98378285978658be508da822c110d5d61e8950872cc75c4296d7ae.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$"] [17592186091935 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2 .$"] [17592186091935 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have $\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7,$ and $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36+7-18 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(5,25).\n$$"] [17592186091936 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2,X_3]^T$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3.$"] [17592186091936 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n![](images/12/129ff41cb0fc5917efdc4f990d3d2efeb7546911eadb52340156f1ddc4f9d92f.svg)\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/id #uuid "33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/math-academy-id "q-159742"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=5\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4=9.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{9}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=6\\pi .$"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf"] ["answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/id #uuid "929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de"] ["answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/value "12\\pi "] ["answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/id #uuid "63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9"] ["answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{6}"] ["answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/id #uuid "bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c"] ["answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/value "4\\pi "] ["answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/id #uuid "f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1"] ["answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/value "6\\pi "] ["answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/id #uuid "a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7"] ["answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/correct "answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/choices "answer-929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/choices "answer-63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/choices "answer-bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/choices "answer-f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1"] ["answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/choices "answer-a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7"] ["question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf"] [17592186092330 :knowledge-point/questions "question-33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad"] ["question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/id #uuid "c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc"] ["question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159744"] ["question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$"] ["question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=20\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot (-6)=64.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{64}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=16\\pi .$"] ["question-c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643"] ["answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/id #uuid "3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07"] ["answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/value "16\\pi "] ["answer-9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbba0c48627c" :answer/id #uuid "9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbb