5-b90b-d6b9b56e65d1"] [17592186092331 :knowledge-point/questions "question-de58daf1-e39b-5442-89f0-7a159aadbac7"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/id #uuid "3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159778"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$\n\n*Hint: Tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1587\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3409\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3372\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3336\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+3\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+3\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =5+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+45-12 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,34).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 5) & =P(\\frac{1-8}{\\sqrt{34}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{5-8}{\\sqrt{34}}) \\\\  & =P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z<-1.20) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z\\le -1.20) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.20) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.51) & =0.3050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20) \\\\  & =0.3050-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.1899.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(1\\le Y\\le 5)=0.1899.\n$$"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c"] ["answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/id #uuid "45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601"] ["answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/value "0.1558"] ["answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/id #uuid "1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b"] ["answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/value "0.1762"] ["answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/id #uuid "e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936"] ["answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/value "0.1899"] ["answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/id #uuid "07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea"] ["answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/value "0.2002"] ["answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/id #uuid "23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15"] ["answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/value "0.2109"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/correct "answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/choices "answer-45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/choices "answer-1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/choices "answer-e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/choices "answer-07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea"] ["answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/choices "answer-23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15"] ["question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c"] [17592186092331 :knowledge-point/questions "question-3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f"] [17592186092560 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$"] [17592186092560 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$"] [17592186092560 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/id #uuid "22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4"] ["answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,13)"] ["answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/id #uuid "2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77"] ["answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,17)"] ["answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/id #uuid "dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6"] ["answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{13})"] ["answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/id #uuid "09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5"] ["answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{15})"] ["answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/id #uuid "9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094"] ["answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{17})"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/correct "answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices "answer-22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices "answer-2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices "answer-dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices "answer-09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5"] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices "answer-9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/id #uuid "485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159747"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}.$"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2+4+2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(3,8).\n$$"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/id #uuid "31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071"] ["answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/id #uuid "1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2"] ["answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/value "Y∼N(1,2)"] ["answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/id #uuid "483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667"] ["answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/value "Y∼N(3,4)"] ["answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/id #uuid "275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8"] ["answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/value "Y∼N(3,8)"] ["answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/id #uuid "b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9"] ["answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/value "Y∼N(6,4)"] ["answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/id #uuid "f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c"] ["answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/value "Y∼N(6,8)"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/correct "answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/choices "answer-1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/choices "answer-483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/choices "answer-275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/choices "answer-b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9"] ["answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/choices "answer-f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c"] ["question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071"] [17592186092331 :knowledge-point/questions "question-485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/id #uuid "a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159906"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3}.$"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+3+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-1+14 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(4,19).\n$$"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/id #uuid "53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775"] ["answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/id #uuid "52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb"] ["answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/value "N(2,21)"] ["answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/id #uuid "458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c"] ["answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/value "N(4,19)"] ["answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/id #uuid "407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8"] ["answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/value "N(4,7)"] ["answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/id #uuid "1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31"] ["answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/value "N(6,19)"] ["answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/id #uuid "b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d"] ["answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/value "N(6,7)"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/correct "answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/choices "answer-52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/choices "answer-458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/choices "answer-407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/choices "answer-1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31"] ["answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/choices "answer-b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d"] ["question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775"] [17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions "question-a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9"] [17592186092581 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y<1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ |"] [17592186092581 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+2-2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+10-1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(3,12).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y<1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<1) & =P(Z<\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58) & =P(Z\\le -0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.58)=0.7190.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.58)=1-Φ(0.58)=1-0.7190=0.2810.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58)=0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y<1)=0.2810.\n$$"] [17592186092581 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] [17592186089657 :answer-field/correct 17592186089659] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/id #uuid "5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86"] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/math-academy-id "q-159788"] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}.$"] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2-2-3 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9-5+60 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-3,64).\n$$"] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/id #uuid "67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369"] ["answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/id #uuid "b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670"] ["answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/value "N(-1,8)"] ["answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/id #uuid "9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3"] ["answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/value "N(-3,64)"] ["answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/id #uuid "bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea"] ["answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/value "N(-3,8)"] ["answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/id #uuid "ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f"] ["answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/value "N(2,64)"] ["answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/id #uuid "a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d"] ["answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/value "N(2,71)"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/correct "answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/choices "answer-b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/choices "answer-9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/choices "answer-bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/choices "answer-ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f"] ["answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/choices "answer-a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d"] ["question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369"] [17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions "question-5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/id #uuid "b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159794"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+9+4 \\\\  & =13,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+42+30 \\\\  & =67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(13,67).\n$$"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18"] ["answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/id #uuid "4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce"] ["answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/value "N(13,51)"] ["answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/id #uuid "0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9"] ["answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/value "N(13,67)"] ["answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/id #uuid "9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc"] ["answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/value "N(17,51)"] ["answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/id #uuid "21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581"] ["answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/value "N(5,34)"] ["answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/id #uuid "ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4"] ["answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/value "N(5,67)"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/correct "answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/choices "answer-4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/choices "answer-0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/choices "answer-9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/choices "answer-21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581"] ["answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/choices "answer-ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4"] ["question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18"] [17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/id #uuid "279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159790"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812"] ["answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/id #uuid "8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a"] ["answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/value "N(-7,11)"] ["answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/id #uuid "212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0"] ["answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/value "N(-7,25)"] ["answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/id #uuid "e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc"] ["answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/value "N(1,25)"] ["answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/id #uuid "96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2"] ["answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/value "N(4,11)"] ["answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/id #uuid "85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657"] ["answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/value "N(4,13)"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/correct "answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/choices "answer-8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/choices "answer-212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/choices "answer-e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/choices "answer-96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2"] ["answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/choices "answer-85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657"] ["question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812"] [17592186092332 :knowledge-point/questions "question-279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb"] [17592186093481 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be two (dependent) random variables. We say that $\\mathbf{X}$ is a **bivariate normal vector** with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ if the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, \n\n- $\\sigma^2_1 = \\textrm{Var}[X_1]$ and $\\sigma^2_2 = \\textrm{Var}[X_2]$ are the variances of $X_1$ and $X_2,$\n\n- $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]$ is the covariance of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n\n- $\\rho$ is the linear correlation coefficient of $X_1$ and $X_2,$ and\n\n- $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma,$ the covariance matrix.\n\nA graph of a typical density function of a bivariate normal vector is depicted below. \n\n![](images/d1/d195358d6dc5b7b8a1080d53c774ee4289ce0f97c17de3bf45a52e86c6b20e29.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is often referred to as having a \"bell shape.\""] [17592186093483 :tutorial/content "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-1}|Σ{|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-2/2}{|Σ|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2),$ we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables."] [17592186093485 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ \n\n- when $\\rho \\to 0,$ the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, while\n\n- when $\\rho \\to 1,$ the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n \n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$\n\n- when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" while\n\n- for larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2,$ the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)"] [17592186093484 :tutorial/content "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as *\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186093482 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T$ with mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T$ with this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors: \n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as \n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\n is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form."] [17592186092422 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0]] "retractions" [[17592186091934 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n$f(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)$,\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$."] [17592186091934 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = (μ_{1}; μ_{2}), Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]\n$$\n\nand $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $\\mid \\Sigma \\mid$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma$.\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho$, our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\Sigma \\mid = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{8}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4 π 2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nk=4\\pi\\sqrt 2\n$$"] [17592186091935 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2$."] [17592186091935 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y$, defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= aμ_{1} + bμ_{2}, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= a^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + b^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + 2abσ_{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= E[3X_{1} + X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3E[X_{1}] + E[X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot μ_{1} + μ_{2} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= Var[3X_{1} + X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3^{2} \\cdot 4 + 1^{2} \\cdot 7 + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&= 36 + 7 - 18 \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$Y \\sim N(5,25)$."] [17592186091936 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2,X_3]^T$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3$."] [17592186091936 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T\n$$\n\nis a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, then the random variable $Y$, defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of\n\n$$\nY = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 - 4 + 4 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 12 - 6 + 56 \\\\\n&= 62\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62)\n$$"] [17592186092579 :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$σ_{1}^{2} = 10, σ_{2}^{2} = 5, σ_{12} = 5$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{k}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ))$,\n\nwhere $μ$ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$."] [17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089648] [17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089649] [17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089650] [17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089646] [17592186089645 :answer-field/choices 17592186089647] [17592186092580 :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nLet $Y = X_{1} - 2X_{2}$. Calculate $P(Y \\ge 1)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7967$ | $0.7995$ | $0.8023$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8238$ | $0.8264$ | $0.8289$ |\n| $1.0$ | $0.8485$ | $0.8508$ | $0.8531$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186092560 :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_{1} - 2X_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089536] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089532] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089533] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089534] [17592186089531 :answer-field/choices 17592186089535] [17592186092581 :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nLet $Y = X_{1} + 2X_{2} - X_{3}$. Find $P(Y < 1)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ | $0.08$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.5$ | $0.7088$ | $0.7123$ | $0.7157$ | $0.7190$ |\n| $0.6$ | $0.7422$ | $0.7454$ | $0.7486$ | $0.7517$ |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7734$ | $0.7764$ | $0.7794$ | $0.7823$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.8023$ | $0.8051$ | $0.8078$ | $0.8106$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186093481 :tutorial/content "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nbe two (dependent) random variables. We say that $\\mathbf{X}$ is a **bivariate normal vector** with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ if the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n- $\\sigma^2_1 = \\textrm{Var}[X_1]$ and $\\sigma^2_2 = \\textrm{Var}[X_2]$ are the variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n- $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]$ is the covariance of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n- $\\rho$ is the linear correlation coefficient of $X_1$ and $X_2$, and\n- $\\mid \\Sigma \\mid$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma$, the covariance matrix.\n\nA graph of a typical density function of a bivariate normal vector is depicted below.\n\n![](images/d1/d195358d6dc5b7b8a1080d53c774ee4289ce0f97c17de3bf45a52e86c6b20e29.png)\n\nThis surface is often referred to as having a \"bell shape.\""] [17592186093483 :tutorial/content "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2} ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{\\mid Σ \\mid}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-1} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-2/2} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2)$, we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables."] [17592186093485 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nwith the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ when $\\rho \\to 0$, the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, whilewhen $\\rho \\to 1$, the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x$.\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" whilefor larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$, the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const}\n$$\n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)$.\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)"] [17592186093484 :tutorial/content "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by*\n> $μ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> *then the random variable $Y$, defined as*\n> $Y= aX_1 + bX_2$\n> *is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b$. Furthermore, we have that*\n> $E[Y]|= aμ_{1} + bμ_{2},; Var[Y]|= a^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + b^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + 2abσ_{12}$."] [17592186093482 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2} ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right)\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution\n\n$$\n\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T\n$$\n\nwith mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue} \\mid \\Sigma \\mid}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p}$, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue} \\mid \\Sigma \\mid}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T\n$$\n\nwith this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, then the random variable $Y$, defined as $Y= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i$ is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form."]]}]